section: quotas
title: Quotas and limits
about: |
  The quotas of the cloud and the floors of Palette: what a build asks for, against what the
  account can give.
rules:
- id: azure-vcpu-quota
  title: The regional vCPU quota covers the pools
  engines:
  - azure
  - aks
  tier: cloud
  severity: fail
  status: inferred
  what: |
    Azure refuses a virtual machine over the regional vCPU quota, or over the quota of the VM
    family, with `OperationNotAllowed`. Palette checks no Azure quota; the docs leave the capacity
    to the operator. The check sums the vCPUs of the pools and compares them with the free
    `cores` of `az vm list-usage`. The world holds no vCPU count for a type, so the check reads
    the number in the type name: `Standard_D4s_v3` is 4, `Standard_DS1_v2` is 1. That reading is
    right for the common families and is not a catalog. On AKS the control plane pool has no
    node. The family quota can be lower than the regional one; the reproduce line prints it.
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - az
    argv:
    - az
    - vm
    - list-usage
    - --location
    - '{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.location }}'
    - --subscription
    - '{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.subscriptionId }}'
    - --output
    - json
    expr: |
      def vcpus: sub("^(Standard_|Basic_)?[A-Za-z]+"; "") | sub("[^0-9].*$"; "") | if . == "" then 0 else tonumber end;
      .cloud as $cloud | .entity.spec.cloudConfig.location as $loc
      | ([ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[] | select(($cloud == "aks" and (.poolConfig.isControlPlane // false)) | not)
           | ((.cloudConfig.instanceType // "") | vcpus) * (.poolConfig.size // 0) ] | add // 0) as $need
      | if .exec.exit != 0 then ["az vm list-usage failed: \(.exec.stderr | .[:200])"]
        else ([ (.exec.stdout // [])[] | select(.name.value == "cores") ] | first) as $cores
          | if $cores == null then ["az vm list-usage gave no cores row for \($loc)"]
            else (($cores.limit // 0) - ($cores.currentValue // 0)) as $free
              | if $need > $free then ["the pools ask for \($need) vCPUs in \($loc) (from the type names), and the regional quota has \($free) free of \($cores.limit) (\($cores.currentValue) in use): Azure refuses the machines with OperationNotAllowed; the family quota of each type can be lower still"] else [] end
            end
        end
  reproduce:
  - az vm list-usage --location {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.location }} --subscription {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.subscriptionId }} --query "[?name.value=='cores' || contains(name.localizedValue, 'Family vCPUs')].[name.localizedValue,currentValue,limit]" --output table
  - az vm list-skus --location {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.location }} --size {{ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[0].cloudConfig.instanceType }} --query '[].[name,family,capabilities[?name==`vCPUs`].value|[0]]' --output table
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Read the usage of the region and the family in the validate route, with the vCPU count of the catalog.
  evidence:
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/quotas/per-vm-quota-requests
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.spectrocloud.com/clusters/public-cloud/azure/create-azure-cluster/
    note: ensure there is sufficient capacity in the preferred Azure region for vCPUs, VNets, and public IP addresses
  - kind: source
    note: name, reserved tags, instance type lookup, and profiles; no quota
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/azure/vcpu-quota-low.json
    clean: fixtures/azure/vcpu-quota-clean.json
- id: vcluster-min
  title: A virtual cluster has 3 cores or more
  engines:
  - all
  tier: static
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  what: |
    Scope: cluster groups and the virtual clusters in them. Palette refuses a CPU limit under 3
    cores, and a memory limit under 3 GiB, in the limit config of a cluster group. A virtual
    cluster asks its size with `instanceType.maxCPU` of its pool. A virtual cluster is not a cloud
    type of strata-preflight, so there is no check here; the reproduce lines read the two sizes.
  reproduce:
  - 'jq ''.spec.machinepoolconfig[].cloudConfig.instanceType'' virtual-cluster.json   # maxCPU must be 3 or more'
  - 'curl -s -H "ApiKey: $PALETTE_API_KEY" -H "ProjectUid: $PALETTE_PROJECT_UID" "${PALETTE_API:-https://api.spectrocloud.com}/v1/clustergroups/<uid>" | jq .spec.clustersConfig.limitConfig'
  fix:
    owner: none
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: teardown lab
  - kind: source
    note: Palette refuses a CPU limit under 3 cores, and a memory limit under 3 GiB or over 100000 GiB
- id: worker-cpu-floor
  title: The workers have the CPU the packs need
  engines:
  - aws
  - gcp
  - gke
  - azure
  - aks
  - maas
  - vsphere
  tier: palette
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  what: |
    The packs of the profiles need a minimum of CPU across the worker nodes: 4.1 cores with the
    lab packs, 5.3 with a monitoring stack. Palette refuses the create with "A minimum allocation
    of '4.1' CPU cores is required across all worker nodes". The validate route answers it in
    seconds, with the machine pools that fall short. On EKS the route reports no CPU floor for a
    managed node group. One `t3.xlarge` worker got `200` with no error; the aws route answers
    `MachinePoolWithMinCpuRequired` for the same pool. No run shows whether the EKS create fails
    later, so on EKS a pass is not a proof. `palette-validate` calls the same route and reports
    every error of it; this rule reads the CPU and memory errors of the pools alone.
  check:
    kind: palette
    method: POST
    path: /v1/spectroclusters/{{ .routeCloud }}/validate
    body: .validateBody
    expr: |
      .response as $r
      | if ($r.ok | not) then [] # a refusal of the whole entity is palette-validate's
        else [ ($r.body.machinePools.results // [])[] | .name as $n | (.errors // [])[]
               | select((.message // "") | test("CPU|memory|cores"; "i"))
               | "pool \($n): \(.message)" ]
        end
  fix:
    owner: provider
    text: Call the validate route at plan time.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: teardown lab
  - kind: run
    ref: preflight-plan eks 20260929T042910Z
    note: the EKS route answered 200 with no pool error for one t3.xlarge worker; the aws route answers MachinePoolWithMinCpuRequired for the same pool
  - kind: source
    note: Palette checks the resources of the machines against what the packs need
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/validate-cpu.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/clean.json
